"""Original defensive architecture decisions, not vendor exam items."""
ITEMS=[]
def family(options, rows):
    assert len(options)==len(rows)==4
    for k,(stem,why) in enumerate(rows):ITEMS.append((stem,[options[k]]+[v for j,v in enumerate(options) if j!=k],why))
family(['Place enforcement by the resource','Refresh the device posture signal','Separate workload trust zones','Reevaluate active access decisions'],[
('Network segmentation already isolates the workload zones, but a gateway authorizes entry without checking access to each sensitive application inside a zone. The requirement is an explicit authorization check for each requested application. Which change directly supplies that missing check?', 'Move enforcement toward individual resources so authorization is not inherited across the segment. Additional posture data alone does not create resource-level boundaries.'),
('Access rules already evaluate each requested resource, but they rely on a device compliance result collected months earlier. Which change fixes the stale input to those decisions?', 'Fresh device posture is needed for a meaningful dynamic decision. Adding more enforcement points would still consume stale evidence.'),
('Two service tiers have correct user authentication but unrestricted east-west communication. A compromised reporting workload can reach a payment database it never needs. Which change directly limits this path?', 'Separate workload trust zones and permit only required flows. User authentication does not itself restrict service-to-service movement.'),
('A session remains authorized after the identity risk score rises sharply. New sessions are correctly blocked under the same policy. What capability is missing for existing access?', 'Ongoing reevaluation can revoke or constrain access when context changes. A check only at session creation leaves the existing session trusted.')])
family(['A policy decision component','A policy enforcement component','An identity evidence provider','An asset posture information feed'],[
('In a zero-trust design, a component combines identity, device condition and resource rules to decide whether a request should be allowed. Which architectural role does it perform?', 'The decision component evaluates policy inputs. Enforcement acts on the resulting decision rather than independently supplying all of those inputs.'),
('A gateway opens or closes a connection according to an authorization decision supplied by another component. Which role is the gateway performing?', 'The enforcement point controls the actual access path. The decision role determines whether the policy permits access.'),
('A service supplies a signed assertion about the authenticated user to an authorization system. It does not determine whether that user may read the requested file. Which role best fits?', 'The identity provider supplies authentication evidence. Resource authorization still requires the relevant policy decision.'),
('An endpoint service reports patch state and integrity measurements to an access engine. It neither authenticates the human user nor opens the connection. Which role is this?', 'The posture feed provides asset-condition evidence. The policy engine consumes that evidence when making access decisions.')])
family(['Attribute-based authorization','Role-based authorization','Owner-controlled authorization','Label-enforced authorization'],[
('A research platform must combine project membership, record sensitivity, device compliance and current location for each access decision. Which authorization model most directly expresses these independent conditions?', 'ABAC combines subject, resource and environmental attributes. A job role alone does not express the changing context described.'),
('A warehouse application gives each employee one of a small set of stable job functions, with permissions managed for those functions. Which model most directly matches this design?', 'RBAC associates permissions with organizational roles. Additional context-dependent conditions would require more than this simple model.'),
('A collaboration system allows each document owner to grant or remove other users’ access at the owner’s discretion. Which model describes that authority?', 'Discretionary access control allows the owner to determine access. Mandatory labels instead constrain access through centrally enforced policy.'),
('A system uses centrally assigned sensitivity labels and user clearances. File owners cannot override the resulting restrictions. Which model fits?', 'Mandatory access control enforces label-based policy independently of an owner’s discretion. Ownership does not authorize an exception.')])
family(['Review source without execution','Exercise a running application','Inspect third-party components','Observe instrumented execution'],[
('A team wants a pre-build check for unsafe code paths in its own source, before a runnable service exists. Which assurance approach fits this stage?', 'Static analysis reviews source or representations without executing the application. Dynamic testing requires a running target.'),
('A tester has an authorized staging URL but no source or instrumentation. The goal is to assess externally observable application behavior. Which approach fits?', 'Dynamic testing exercises the running application from its interfaces. It does not require source access or internal instrumentation.'),
('A release contains several open-source libraries. The immediate question is whether the selected dependency versions have known vulnerabilities. Which approach targets that question?', 'Software composition analysis examines third-party components and versions. Reviewing only first-party source does not establish dependency vulnerability status.'),
('A test environment can collect application internals while realistic requests execute. The team wants to link observed requests to the code paths handling them. Which approach fits?', 'Interactive analysis uses instrumentation during execution. Pure external dynamic testing lacks that same internal visibility.')])
family(['Gate changes through peer review','Stage a limited canary rollout','Run regression checks','Verify release provenance'],[
('An unauthorized direct push bypassed required review on a release branch. Which CI/CD control directly addresses the bypass?', 'Protected change gates enforce review before integration. Runtime canaries detect deployment effects rather than authorize source changes.'),
('A reviewed update may interact unexpectedly with real traffic. The team wants to expose only a small controlled population before wider release. Which technique fits?', 'A canary deployment limits initial exposure and supports observation before expansion. Regression checks alone do not reproduce all production behavior.'),
('A fix changes a shared authorization module. The team must confirm that previously working permission scenarios still behave correctly. Which check answers this?', 'Regression tests look for unintended changes to established behavior. Release provenance identifies origin rather than functional correctness.'),
('A repository contains an approved source revision, but the deployment team must establish that the received package was produced by the authorized build process. Which check is most relevant?', 'Provenance connects the artifact to its source and build process. A source review alone does not prove the deployed artifact came from that process.')])
family(['A component inventory artifact','A security requirement baseline','A release integrity signature','A vulnerability disclosure process'],[
('An acquirer needs a structured list of the libraries included in a delivered application so it can identify affected products after a dependency advisory. Which artifact supports this?', 'A software bill of materials identifies components and relationships. An integrity signature does not list the contents.'),
('Before accepting bids, a buyer needs all software suppliers to understand the required authentication, logging and support capabilities. Which item establishes comparable expectations?', 'A security requirements baseline communicates the acceptance expectations. A component inventory records what was delivered rather than what was required.'),
('A distributor needs recipients to verify that a downloaded release was signed by its authorized publisher and has not changed. Which mechanism provides that evidence?', 'A digital signature provides origin and integrity evidence when the verification key is trusted. It does not prove absence of vulnerabilities.'),
('Researchers need an established channel and handling process for reporting flaws in a deployed product. Which supplier capability meets this need?', 'A vulnerability disclosure process enables reports to be received and addressed. A signed release alone does not provide a reporting workflow.')])
family(['Reduce exposed service functions','Add independent security layers','Centralize useful audit records','Measure the control outcome'],[
('A server exposes two unused management services. The approved workload needs neither, and the team wants to remove unnecessary entry points. Which design action addresses the stated goal?', 'Remove unnecessary functions to reduce attack surface. More monitoring can detect misuse but leaves the entry points available.'),
('A sensitive application depends entirely on one gateway control. Designers want a compromise of that gateway not to remove every protection for the data. Which principle should guide the change?', 'Independent layers provide defense in depth. Repeating the same dependency in several places may still leave a single failure mode.'),
('An investigation requires correlating identity, gateway and application events, but records exist only on separate local hosts. Which architecture change most directly supports correlation?', 'Central collection of relevant audit records enables correlation across components. It must preserve useful context and access controls.'),
('A new exfiltration control is deployed. Managers need evidence that it catches the intended transfers while preserving authorized work. Which activity evaluates effectiveness?', 'Measure detection and permitted-work outcomes against the goal. Installation completion alone is not proof of effectiveness.')])
family(['A resource-specific service identity','A time-limited privileged role','A federated workforce identity','A separate emergency account'],[
('A scheduled reporting job must read one storage location without using an employee’s password. Which identity design best fits its ongoing nonhuman role?', 'A narrowly scoped service identity separates workload access from a human account. An emergency account is not intended for routine automation.'),
('An engineer needs elevated rights for a short approved maintenance window, then should return to ordinary permissions automatically. Which design fits?', 'A time-limited privileged role supports just-in-time elevation. A permanent broad service identity would not match the temporary human task.'),
('Employees already authenticate through an enterprise identity provider. A new SaaS application should accept that managed identity lifecycle instead of separate local passwords. Which design fits?', 'Federation lets the SaaS rely on the enterprise identity provider. The service still needs authorization appropriate to the application.'),
('The primary identity provider may be unavailable during recovery. A tightly controlled, audited access path must work independently for essential administrators. Which design addresses this dependency?', 'A separate emergency account can provide controlled break-glass access. It should be protected, tested and monitored rather than used routinely.')])
family(['Identity proofing before issuance','Certificate signing by the CA','Revocation-status validation','Hostname identity validation'],[
('A registration workflow receives a certificate request for a newly acquired company. Before issuance, the operator must establish that the requester is entitled to the claimed identity. Which activity is needed?', 'Registration identity proofing validates the claim before a CA issues the certificate. Checking an existing certificate’s revocation status answers a different question.'),
('A certificate request has passed identity validation. Which PKI operation creates the issuer’s verifiable binding between the subject and its public key?', 'The CA signs the certificate binding. Identity proofing supports issuance but does not itself create the signed certificate.'),
('A certificate chains to a trusted issuer and is within its dates, but the holder’s private key was reported compromised. Which additional validation addresses this issue?', 'Revocation status can indicate that an otherwise time-valid certificate should no longer be trusted. Matching the hostname is not enough.'),
('A browser receives a valid certificate for an unrelated service from a trusted issuer. Which check should reject its use for the requested website?', 'The requested hostname must match the certificate’s authenticated identity. A trusted issuer does not make a certificate valid for every hostname.')])
family(['An inline traffic control','An API-based content review','A cloud configuration assessment','A workload runtime sensor'],[
('A cloud security requirement calls for blocking prohibited uploads before they reach a SaaS service. The approved architecture routes those uploads through an inspection point. Which control placement fits?', 'Inline inspection can enforce a decision before forwarding the upload. A later API content review is normally retrospective.'),
('The security team must inspect existing files already stored in an approved SaaS tenant, including uploads made before the control was installed. Which approach can reach that stored content?', 'An API integration can inspect existing tenant content through supported service APIs. An inline path only sees traffic that passes through it.'),
('An organization wants to find storage resources whose public-access settings diverge from policy across its cloud accounts. Which assessment targets this condition?', 'Cloud posture assessment evaluates resource configurations. Runtime process monitoring does not by itself establish storage policy compliance.'),
('A container service needs evidence of unexpected process execution inside running workloads. Which sensor provides the most direct observation?', 'A runtime workload sensor observes process behavior. A configuration snapshot cannot show every action during execution.')])
family(['Constrain the tenant access policy','Limit the application permission','Protect the application data key','Record the administrative changes'],[
('A storage service is correctly encrypted, but its tenant policy permits public reads of confidential objects. Which change directly removes the identified exposure?', 'Correct the access policy. Encryption at rest does not prevent a service from returning plaintext to a publicly authorized request.'),
('A serverless function needs to read one queue but has broad management permissions over the account. Which change targets the excess authority?', 'Limit the function’s permissions to its required operations and resources. A stronger data key does not reduce its management privileges.'),
('A threat model assumes storage administrators can obtain encrypted backups. The application’s separate data key must remain unavailable to those administrators. Which control boundary is central?', 'Protect the data-encryption key separately from the storage administrators. Storage access restrictions alone do not address the assumed backup access.'),
('An organization needs to investigate who altered a cloud resource’s policy. Preventive permissions are already in place. Which capability supplies the missing historical evidence?', 'Administrative audit records show policy-change events and actors. Current policy settings alone do not reconstruct who changed them.')])
family(['Isolate independent failure zones','Distribute stateless request load','Preserve session state externally','Test restoration from backup'],[
('Two service replicas run in the same facility and depend on the same power distribution. The requirement is to survive loss of that facility. Which design change addresses the shared failure?', 'Separate failure zones reduce dependence on one facility. More replicas within the same failure domain do not meet the stated requirement.'),
('A stateless API tier reaches its per-instance processing limit. Additional independent instances can serve any request. Which design meets the increased demand?', 'Horizontal distribution across instances spreads the request load. Backup restoration does not add live request capacity.'),
('A shopping application keeps its only cart state inside one web process. A process replacement loses active carts. Which change best supports replaceable front-end instances?', 'Move necessary state to an appropriately resilient external store. Adding stateless replicas cannot recover state held only in a failed process.'),
('A team has redundant live databases, but an accidental authorized deletion replicated to every copy. Which existing capability must be tested to recover earlier valid data?', 'A tested historical backup can recover a prior valid state. Live replication also propagates logical mistakes and is not a complete backup substitute.')])
family(['Minimize retained sensitive fields','Label the data by sensitivity','Restrict copies in test systems','Track the final disposal evidence'],[
('An application collects a sensitive field that no approved feature or retention obligation requires. Which design change removes this unnecessary exposure at its source?', 'Data minimization avoids retaining data with no justified purpose. Labeling still leaves the unnecessary sensitive value stored.'),
('Automated transfer rules cannot distinguish public reports from confidential ones because records have no reliable sensitivity metadata. Which change enables classification-aware enforcement?', 'Sensitivity labels provide the metadata needed by handling controls. Disposal records do not classify active information.'),
('Developers troubleshoot with full production datasets copied to broadly accessible test environments. The same diagnostics can use masked or synthetic data. Which design boundary should change?', 'Restrict sensitive copies and use suitable minimized test data. Production access controls do not automatically follow an exported copy.'),
('A decommissioning plan requires proof that retained media was sanitized under the approved method. Which lifecycle record supports closure?', 'Disposal evidence documents execution of the approved sanitization requirement. Removing inventory entries alone does not establish sanitization.')])
family(['Separate approval from deployment','Limit the build-runner identity','Validate third-party build inputs','Isolate untrusted test execution'],[
('One developer can change a critical release, approve it and deploy it without another authorized reviewer. Which pipeline change directly introduces independent authorization?', 'Separate approval from change and deployment responsibilities. A restricted runner helps containment but does not provide independent review.'),
('A build job needs access to one artifact repository but inherits administrative permissions across production. Which change reduces the potential impact of a compromised job?', 'Scope the runner identity to required resources and actions. Independent review does not justify unnecessary runtime privileges.'),
('A pipeline downloads a dependency using a mutable reference. The approved review must bind to the actual component version and origin used for the build. Which control targets this gap?', 'Validate and pin inputs with trustworthy provenance and integrity evidence. Runner isolation alone does not establish what dependency was selected.'),
('A CI service executes proposed changes from untrusted contributors. Those jobs must not reach production secrets or privileged release workers. Which boundary is essential?', 'Isolate untrusted execution from secrets and privileged workers. Valid source syntax does not make submitted code trustworthy.')])
family(['Require context-aware reauthentication','Scope the partner resource grant','Revoke the former worker session','Audit the privileged transaction'],[
('A valid employee session requests a substantially more sensitive function from an unfamiliar device. Policy requires renewed assurance for this context change. Which access action fits?', 'Context-aware reauthentication obtains the required assurance before the sensitive action. Merely logging the request does not meet the policy.'),
('A partner needs one reporting API but should not inherit access to all internal applications after authentication. Which authorization choice addresses the boundary?', 'Grant access specifically to the needed resource and operations. Authentication proves identity but does not justify blanket authorization.'),
('A worker’s employment has ended. New logins are blocked, but an existing long-lived session still reaches confidential services. Which action closes the remaining access?', 'Invalidate the active session or its authorization so deprovisioning covers existing access. Blocking only new logins leaves the old session usable.'),
('A privileged operation is validly authorized and completed. The organization must later establish the actor, target, time and outcome. Which capability addresses that requirement?', 'A protected audit trail records the transaction context. Reauthentication alone does not create a complete operation history.')])
family(['A four-hour recovery objective','A fifteen-minute data-loss objective','A six-hour maximum outage limit','A two-hour measured restoration'],[
('A business specifies that a service should be restored within four hours after disruption. Which metric expresses the planned restoration target?', 'The recovery time objective is the target restoration duration. It differs from the maximum tolerable outage and actual measured recovery.'),
('A database recovery plan permits losing no more than fifteen minutes of committed updates. Which stated metric describes the acceptable recovery point?', 'The recovery point objective limits the age of recoverable data. It does not describe how long restoration may take.'),
('Business impact analysis concludes that an outage beyond six hours would cause unacceptable mission harm, even though the restoration target is shorter. Which metric captures this outer boundary?', 'Maximum tolerable outage describes the business limit. The recovery objective should support recovery within that boundary.'),
('During an exercise, service was actually restored two hours after the disruption began. Which value records the observed performance rather than a planned limit?', 'Measured restoration time is an observed exercise result. It should be compared with the planned recovery objective, not substituted for the objective itself.')])
assert len(ITEMS)==64
